\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Integrated a homogeneous shear/density system and showed expansion damping reverses toward the past for w less than one; no isotropic-singularity mechanism.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
Read the supplied definition, catalogue wording, source qualifications and reference entries for 30004801. The historical source/context and inherited status are retained; no fresh literature or novelty certification is claimed. No external theorem is used without proof in this attempt; explicit model assumptions are distinguished from facts about the source model.

\paragraph{Testing homogeneous damping toward the past.} In a homogeneous model with mean scale factor \(a(t)>0\), write \(H=\dot a/a\). Suppose a shear component \(\sigma\) and perfect-fluid density \(\rho>0\) satisfy the usual reduced evolution equations \(\dot\sigma+3H\sigma=0\), \(\dot\rho+3H(1+w)\rho=0\), for constant equation-of-state parameter \(w\). Integration, without a perturbative approximation, gives
\[
 \sigma=\sigma_0(a/a_0)^{-3},\qquad
 \rho=\rho_0(a/a_0)^{-3(1+w)},\qquad
 \frac{\sigma^2}{\rho}=\frac{\sigma_0^2}{\rho_0}(a/a_0)^{3(w-1)}.
\]
Thus for nonzero initial shear and \(w<1\), shear grows relative to matter as \(a\downarrow0\). For \(w=1\) the ratio is constant; for \(w>1\) it decays in this reduced calculation.
\paragraph{Mechanism audit.} Damping of shear during expansion therefore cannot simply be run backward to explain isotropic initial singularities. Exactly zero shear stays zero, but imposing it is a special-data condition rather than a dynamical explanation of emergence from general data. The equations were stated as assumptions of this homogeneous test; no existence or stability theorem for an inhomogeneous Einstein--matter solution follows.
\paragraph{Source gap.} A mechanism for vanishing Weyl curvature must also control spatial gradients, anisotropic stresses and the geometric singular limit. The first gap is identifying a matter/data principle and proving that its admissible perturbations approach the isotropic regime, rather than only selecting an exactly symmetric solution. No unique cosmological origin or general initial-data selection is claimed.

\paragraph{Verification and adversarial audit.}
Checked all three w regimes and zero shear; kept the reduced equations as explicit assumptions rather than a full inhomogeneous theorem. This is a GPT-6 Astra Ultra self-audit, not independent human verification. Prompt A drafting followed by prompt B scope/dependency/boundary self-audit. Checked all three w regimes and zero shear; kept the reduced equations as explicit assumptions rather than a full inhomogeneous theorem. The remaining gap is: Specify and prove a matter/initial-data principle controlling anisotropy and spatial curvature near the singularity under general perturbations. Unresolved status and zero original-target completion are retained.

Coordinator checked the two homogeneous evolution integrations and the relative shear exponent in all three equation-of-state regimes. This additional check was model-assisted, not independent human verification.

\paragraph{Tools, process and contributions.}
Python 3 and local file-reading/writing tools for manuscript serialization and structural checks; mathematical calculations were symbolic reasoning, with no numerical experiment or theorem prover. Contribution prompt A was followed by prompt B. GPT-6 Astra with ultra reasoning drafted the research and performed its stated self-audit; any additional coordinator contribution is identified in the verification and audit. A GPT-6-based Codex coordinator assembled the manuscript and checked repository integration; its exact variant and reasoning configuration were not exposed. No human mathematical contribution, independent human proof check or formal proof certification is supplied. Complete derivations and the consulted source references are included here; private reasoning transcripts are not published.

\paragraph{Final status and first remaining gap.}
\textbf{UNRESOLVED}. Specify and prove a matter/initial-data principle controlling anisotropy and spatial curvature near the singularity under general perturbations.

